 investigation='Write a two-part main report: first the eight/twelve versus nine/twelve rates and one-case descriptive gain; then the three conflict-related B failures and absence of a user study. Do not calculate a conflict-slice rate because its denominator is missing. Separately draft the picture’s nine/twelve versus ten/twelve support result and repair the unmeasured claim about every student. Keep all arithmetic as supplied-case description, not statistical significance. Record actual local writing/calculation or paper trace; no model, learner study or timing is run. Reserve the independent fifteen-case report for its first attempt.',
 teacher_key='Main A 8/12 ≈ 66.667%, B 9/12 = 75%, gain 1/12 ≈ 8.333 percentage points. All three B failures concern conflicting sources, but total conflict cases are unknown, so no conflict-slice rate follows. No user study supports classroom usefulness. Separate picture A support 9/12 = 75%, B 10/12 ≈ 83.333%, same one-case gain but a different stated outcome. No learning or timing claim follows. Independent candidate 10/15 ≈ 66.667%, baseline 9/15 = 60%, descriptive gain 1/15 ≈ 6.667 points. State no timings measured and no established population/significance or broad task generalization. Accept other concrete supported limits without inventing missing observations.',
 reference='Results report actual counts, denominators, measured outcomes and descriptive magnitude before interpretation. Main A passes 8/12 ≈ 66.667% and B 9/12 = 75%, gain one of twelve or about 8.333 percentage points. Three B failures involve conflicting sources. Their total slice denominator is unknown, so no conflict-slice rate can be inferred. No user study occurred. Separate picture measures answer support on twelve fixed questions from one approved collection: A 9/12 = 75%, B 10/12 ≈ 83.333%, again one supported-answer gain. These are distinct scenarios and outcomes. Support is not learner improvement; a claim every student becomes smarter substitutes an unmeasured outcome and population. No timings, significance or stable population effect are supplied. Preserve failed conditions and the original primary result, and identify precisely what evidence a broader question would need. A limitation does not turn an unsupported claim into a supported one.',
